WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.334
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.334 --> 00:00:04.750
SERGE LACHAPELLE:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:09.110
My name is Serge Lachapelle,
and for the past 10 years,

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:11.510
I've been a product
manager at Google.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:13.480
I'm based out of
a country called

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.810
Sweden, which apparently is very
popular around here these days.

00:00:19.768 --> 00:00:24.940
[LAUGHS] I've had the amazing
opportunity to be part

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.460
of a journey, and--

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:28.840
throughout these 10 years.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:35.260
And this journey, of course,
wasn't done only by myself,

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:37.570
but with many
colleagues that are here

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:41.380
in the room with me today and
for the duration of those 10

00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:42.410
years.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.550
So for everyone, please wave.

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:46.810
There you go.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:50.080
And some-- I'll
invite them upfront,

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.250
those that can help in
answering your questions later.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:58.330
So a bunch of the
people here in the room

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:00.910
have helped build what I'm
going to present here today.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.660
Let's take a look back
at 2007, 10 years ago.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:13.780
A decade feels like a longer
time than if you say 10 years.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.940
And can anyone here
in the room tell me

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.120
something that happened in 2007?

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.320
Do you remember?

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:21.820
The iPhone.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:23.416
Anything else?

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:24.670
AUDIENCE: George Bush.

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:29.650
SERGE LACHAPELLE: Yeah,
George Bush, another good one.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:33.040
My son reminded me, it
was the last Harry Potter

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.300
book was published then.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:38.800
But what I thought of
as well was the iPhone.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.490
And if you look at
the iPhone today,

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:44.950
you can see that a
lot of the design

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.160
aspects of the
iPhone in 2007 have

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:54.710
kept, which is a true
hallmark of the design,

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:57.110
but we all know that
behind the scenes,

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.460
the services that power, the
infrastructure that power all

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.940
the software, all the hardware,
has changed tremendously.

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:08.560
And that made me think of
Google in 2007, when I joined.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:13.300
And this is how the home
page greeted you in 2007.

00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:18.340
And the font's a bit dated.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:21.000
The logo, we know, has changed.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:24.540
But if I was to
switch to 2017, you'll

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.960
notice that even the
same words are used--

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:32.650
Advertising, business, About,
Privacy Terms, Settings.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:34.950
The layout-- the I Feel
Lucky is still there,

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:39.450
the microphone's new,
but things have--

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:42.300
things have been
fairly consistent.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:45.300
But as we all know,
Google, behind the scenes,

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.730
has changed a lot,
as well, in 10 years.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:55.620
And if we look at how
Google was in 2007, well,

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:02.070
Google had just gone from
being 3,000 employees in 2004

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:06.420
to 17,000 employees
by the end of 2007.

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:08.460
So when I joined,
I joined at a--

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.530
just at the cusp of a
period of extreme growth.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:17.940
And today, Google is
about 71,000 employees--

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.050
that was end of year 2016.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:27.130
And what was different
about how Google grew

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:30.070
was that Google grew globally.

00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:33.850
And we didn't just open
offices in countries

00:03:33.850 --> 00:03:36.220
for having a sales presence.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:39.910
We opened offices to have
an engineering presence.

00:03:39.910 --> 00:03:43.750
And so back in
2007, we already had

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:50.530
42 offices across the world,
and not just sales presence

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.720
in the countries to
do local business,

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:54.760
but engineering presence.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.430
So Google was one of the-- is
one of the few companies that

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:00.820
really invested in a
global engineering presence

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:04.330
from very early on.

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:06.520
Talent was global.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:10.690
We wanted products to be
made closer to our users

00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:12.610
and have that input,
so it's really

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:15.910
helped at building a very
diverse engineering workforce.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:25.850
So there we were in all these
offices, company growing,

00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:29.240
and everyone very ambitious
to build great products

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:33.350
and to transform the world,
and to change the world.

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:37.180
But we couldn't do it
the traditional way.

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.260
The traditional way of
doing global engineering

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:45.830
means very heavy processes, very
long planning cycles, things

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:48.020
that you can't change on a whim.

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:51.470
And maybe I should have
showed concrete instead

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:54.350
of a bunch of wires tangled.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:58.880
The word "process," in my
head, always means this.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.990
But we wanted to be able
to work globally, but still

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:06.500
be creative, be
innovative, be agile.

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:11.200
You will hear that word
often in engineering.

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:12.400
We tried these guys--

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.660
we tried the phone.

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:17.800
Every employee had a phone
bridge number and a PIN.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:19.760
We hated it.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:21.350
It was bad quality.

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:24.210
It was hard to know when
it's your turn to speak.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:25.160
There's no queues.

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.290
How do you know when it's
your turn to come in?

00:05:27.290 --> 00:05:31.250
You have these long,
awkward silences,

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:34.160
and it was just too hard
to understand people,

00:05:34.160 --> 00:05:40.670
and it didn't promote
creativity at all.

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:47.270
And then I took a snapshot
of my inbox last night, and--

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:49.130
maybe I don't need to
explain too much here,

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:51.590
but email is great.

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:53.930
We overuse email.

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.150
We get overloaded by email.

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:58.970
It's sometimes hard to
get answers quickly,

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:04.790
and it's also very hard
to understand each other.

00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:08.660
And when you're a global
workforce, spoken speech,

00:06:08.660 --> 00:06:12.350
written speech, tone,
what you actually mean,

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:14.630
how people perceive
what you mean

00:06:14.630 --> 00:06:18.020
gets tricky in written form.

00:06:18.020 --> 00:06:23.360
And so while we
got far with email,

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:24.860
it didn't carry us to our goals.

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:30.470
So one thing caught
on at Google.

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:34.190
And very early on,
video conferencing

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:38.240
seemed like a great way
to distribute a workforce.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:42.770
It seemed like a great way to
promote agility, creativity,

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:45.065
and to build that team feeling.

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:52.250
So Google's founders, really
early on, decided to go for it,

00:06:52.250 --> 00:06:56.060
and they went for
it in a big way.

00:06:56.060 --> 00:06:59.240
Google started to deploy
Tandberg's video conferencing

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:00.920
units across the world.

00:07:03.440 --> 00:07:08.260
Here's how a room
looked like in 2007.

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:10.810
This was a typical Google room.

00:07:10.810 --> 00:07:13.900
This one was from
our Zurich office.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.540
And you can see
the mess of wires

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:19.340
on the table, the camera
bolted to the wall.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:21.940
All of the technology
was in the black cupboard

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:25.600
there on the left
side of the screen.

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:27.400
I don't know what
was in the fridge

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:31.030
next to it, that
little gray fridge,

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:34.100
but let's just say it was water.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:35.860
[LAUGHS]

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:38.440
But a room like this
would cost anywhere

00:07:38.440 --> 00:07:43.445
between $40,000 and $80,000
in equipment and installation

00:07:43.445 --> 00:07:43.945
costs.

00:07:46.249 --> 00:07:48.040
Remember, every time
someone has to come up

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:51.500
with a drill, that's when
the installation costs start

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:53.530
to climb--

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.150
to climb heavily.

00:07:55.150 --> 00:07:58.390
But we had a lot of these units.

00:07:58.390 --> 00:08:04.930
By the end of 2007, we
had over 2,400 rooms

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:06.370
like this around the world.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:13.550
And these were powered
by infrastructure--

00:08:13.550 --> 00:08:17.360
complex, on-premise
infrastructure.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:20.180
And this was not for
the faint of heart.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:24.530
On the left side of the
screen, you'll see a MCU.

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:28.420
This is where all these
rooms would connect into.

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:30.580
Every one of these--

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:33.200
an MCU, by the way, is a
Multipoint Control Unit,

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:34.580
but it's really a bridge.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:37.309
It's something that mixes all
the video and audio together.

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:44.470
And we had a massive mix of
these MCUs, these gatekeepers,

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:47.620
these failover gatekeepers,
these border controllers,

00:08:47.620 --> 00:08:52.120
to allow meetings with external,
outside of Google, meetings.

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:58.490
And as I said, this was
not for the faint of heart.

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:03.520
Every one of these MCUs could
connect 480 rooms dialing in,

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:08.230
each room using up one port.

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:14.490
Each port cost $4,000 if you
wanted to have 720p video.

00:09:19.630 --> 00:09:21.160
We got really good at this.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:23.560
Imagine being able
to deploy 2,400

00:09:23.560 --> 00:09:26.980
of these rooms-- the world's
biggest Tandberg deployment,

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:29.440
not only in the amount
of rooms deployed,

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:31.990
but the amount of minutes used.

00:09:31.990 --> 00:09:35.140
The systems were used
around the clock.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:37.300
We used them to
have team meetings.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:41.750
We used them to have our
Scrums, or our agile standups.

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:44.770
Using video
conferencing informally

00:09:44.770 --> 00:09:48.070
became a part of our culture
because these systems

00:09:48.070 --> 00:09:50.620
were everywhere.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:52.910
And yes, you even got
to meet your boss, who

00:09:52.910 --> 00:09:55.960
was around the world
somewhere else every week,

00:09:55.960 --> 00:09:57.520
and look him in the
eyes, or look her

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:02.470
in the eyes, every
day, if you wanted.

00:10:02.470 --> 00:10:05.080
So slowly but surely,
VC made Google

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:07.120
more nimble on a global scale.

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:12.510
And then we broke it.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:15.660
We had to go and
ruin a great thing.

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:18.420
We ruined it because it
couldn't grow with us.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:22.380
We had reached the
maximum capacity of what

00:10:22.380 --> 00:10:25.440
a system like this could do.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:30.240
And we tried, but people
wanted to dial in from home.

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:33.870
The software that we used
to dial in from home--

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:35.790
it was written by the
same people that had

00:10:35.790 --> 00:10:38.449
written those conference rooms.

00:10:38.449 --> 00:10:39.990
Those conference
rooms were connected

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:41.670
with dedicated ethernet.

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:43.740
They had a dedicated
part of the network.

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:47.880
They had quality of
service monitoring.

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:51.420
When you're at home, on your
home Wi-Fi or your DSL line,

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:53.506
the conditions are
completely different.

00:10:53.506 --> 00:10:54.630
So software couldn't just--

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:56.134
just couldn't connect properly.

00:10:56.134 --> 00:10:57.300
The calls would get dropped.

00:10:57.300 --> 00:11:00.180
The audio, the
video were horrible.

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:02.430
Everyone dialing from
home would use up

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:06.200
one of those $4,000 ports.

00:11:06.200 --> 00:11:09.470
So then you'd have 15
people dialing in from home,

00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:11.120
and if there's no
more ports available,

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:13.700
it means your 20-person
room that you just

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:16.610
paid $80,000 to install
couldn't connect to the meeting,

00:11:16.610 --> 00:11:20.300
because all the home folks
would compete with them.

00:11:20.300 --> 00:11:22.890
These were real issues.

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:25.900
I remember these bridges--

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:29.480
I remember one of
them catching fire.

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:33.080
And we knew-- everyone knew how
expensive those things were.

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:35.930
Everyone knew that Google just
had meetings, after meetings,

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:37.040
after meetings.

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:40.150
So I remember-- this
could be a legend,

00:11:40.150 --> 00:11:43.760
but I remember a Google
field tech having oven mitts,

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:47.090
pulling out a card that was
on fire to save the bridge,

00:11:47.090 --> 00:11:51.770
instead of going at it
with an extinguisher.

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:55.040
That's dedication.

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:58.160
And again, if one of
those cabinets filled up,

00:11:58.160 --> 00:11:59.990
and you only had
four ports left,

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:03.410
and you had a 10-person meeting,
things would fail inexplicably.

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:10.320
I also remember having
to have engineers

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.450
from the various companies
we were buying products

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:15.420
from on site in California.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:23.430
And they were at our offices
for very long periods of time,

00:12:23.430 --> 00:12:25.350
probably charging by the hour.

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:27.780
And I'll always remember
them, because they were always

00:12:27.780 --> 00:12:31.590
the ones renting the fancy
rental cars at the airport--

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:32.730
you know, the big orange--

00:12:32.730 --> 00:12:36.210
or the big orange Mustang
that's always there?

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:39.540
And it says, it could be
yours for $150 more a day.

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:42.480
These engineers that we
were flying in to help us,

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:44.815
they would always drive
the fancy Mustangs.

00:12:44.815 --> 00:12:46.440
And they would work,
and work, and work

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:49.260
at trying to get
our system to scale.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:52.870
And we couldn't get there.

00:12:52.870 --> 00:12:58.180
So what happened?

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:00.940
We decided that it
was time to innovate.

00:13:00.940 --> 00:13:03.610
So Google-- back then,
the management team

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:05.710
was called the
Operating Committee.

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:08.260
And they made an
important decision.

00:13:08.260 --> 00:13:11.900
They said, we're not going
to buy this stuff anymore.

00:13:11.900 --> 00:13:12.400
It's done.

00:13:12.400 --> 00:13:13.450
It's over.

00:13:13.450 --> 00:13:16.820
Google is going to go from
17,000 employees to much,

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:18.280
much more, and
we're probably going

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:22.630
to grow quicker than what
these systems can offer anyhow.

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:24.400
We need to build at scale.

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:28.630
And this means that
the pressure was on.

00:13:28.630 --> 00:13:31.600
So what follows is a
bit of storytelling

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:36.250
about how different
innovation tracks happened--

00:13:36.250 --> 00:13:40.120
some simultaneously,
some serially,

00:13:40.120 --> 00:13:43.550
and some building off
on top of each other.

00:13:43.550 --> 00:13:45.190
The first one was
to build for scale.

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:51.600
So here is video chat in Gmail.

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:54.150
Let's see if we
can get some audio.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:57.150
I did this video
in November 2008--

00:13:57.150 --> 00:13:58.554
took me eight hours.

00:13:58.554 --> 00:13:59.220
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:13:59.220 --> 00:13:59.370
- Ah!

00:13:59.370 --> 00:13:59.970
There you are.

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:03.030
Hi, I'm Serge, and I help
develop Gmail voice and video

00:14:03.030 --> 00:14:04.560
chat from Sweden.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.780
Our team works closely
with the team in the US,

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:09.360
so video chat comes
in really handy.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:12.360
Some things are just better
communicated face-to-face,

00:14:12.360 --> 00:14:16.270
where you can see
expressions, gestures,

00:14:16.270 --> 00:14:19.500
and I can point things
out on a diagram.

00:14:19.500 --> 00:14:23.580
Plus, whenever I travel,
I get to see my kids.

00:14:23.580 --> 00:14:25.410
So let me show you
how this works.

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:28.290
Gmail voice and video chat is
integrated right into Gmail,

00:14:28.290 --> 00:14:30.210
so it's very easy to use.

00:14:30.210 --> 00:14:32.400
To start a voice or
video chat, simply click

00:14:32.400 --> 00:14:36.120
on the "Video &amp; more" link at
the bottom of the chat window.

00:14:36.120 --> 00:14:39.720
When a person receives a
call, they'll hear a ring,

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:42.960
and they'll see a notification
that they can choose to answer.

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:45.960
You can pop out the chat
window and adjust the size

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:47.700
and positioning as you like.

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:50.970
You can even make it go full
screen through a single click.

00:14:50.970 --> 00:14:53.370
It's free and easy to use.

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:56.810
Visit gmail.com/videochat
to get started.

00:14:56.810 --> 00:14:57.395
[END PLAYBACK]

00:14:57.395 --> 00:14:58.770
SERGE LACHAPELLE:
If ever someone

00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:02.670
asks you to volunteer
for something like that,

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:03.850
think twice.

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:07.680
[LAUGHS] What's important here--

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:09.990
so I had been working with
software video conferencing

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:11.040
for 10 years.

00:15:11.040 --> 00:15:13.230
So I started around '97.

00:15:13.230 --> 00:15:16.530
And we had a small
company, and in 10 years,

00:15:16.530 --> 00:15:20.340
we were able to get 200,000
users, and we were all proud.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:24.330
When we launched this
thing, the 200,000 user mark

00:15:24.330 --> 00:15:26.790
passed in a few hours.

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:30.930
It is amazing, the scale
at which Google operates.

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:35.640
And having all these users, with
all these different firewall

00:15:35.640 --> 00:15:37.590
settings, and all these
different networks,

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:40.500
and all these different
cameras and audio card,

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:45.030
and trying to support all of
them was an amazing challenge.

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:47.740
But we learned a ton from it.

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:50.100
So building at that
scale helped us

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:57.470
understand how to cater to
many, many, many scenarios.

00:15:57.470 --> 00:16:00.050
Let's see if I can
go forward here.

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:02.970
Oh, I'm on-- sorry about that.

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:05.797
I need to click on the
white area-- perfect.

00:16:05.797 --> 00:16:07.260
Oops.

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:12.420
So we started with Gmail video
chat, and then we went to--

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:15.870
oops, the video started
playing in the background.

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:17.840
Please don't make me
hear myself again.

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:23.670
[LAUGHS] And this was all fine--
one-on-one conversations--

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:26.170
but we wanted to replace
the Tandberg experience.

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:29.040
We wanted to give
group video meetings.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:37.560
So we have an engineer in
Sweden and a UX designer

00:16:37.560 --> 00:16:40.440
who was based out of our
Kirkland office, Jake Knapp--

00:16:40.440 --> 00:16:42.450
they had been thinking
a lot about this.

00:16:42.450 --> 00:16:45.450
And it was pretty obvious very
quickly that we wouldn't be

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:48.840
able to do full-blown group
video conferencing inside

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:51.330
of Gmail, so we did a little--

00:16:51.330 --> 00:16:53.580
we did a little
sandbox play area

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:57.370
where we started to play with
the software in different ways.

00:16:57.370 --> 00:17:02.160
And these two individuals
came to the epiphany

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:05.940
that, hey, why don't we
just tie and actual meeting,

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:08.819
or a meeting room, to a URL?

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:13.710
And so we built a prototype,
and we called it go/meet,

00:17:13.710 --> 00:17:15.930
and we'd say, hey,
let's go meet in foo--

00:17:15.930 --> 00:17:17.310
go/meet/foo.

00:17:17.310 --> 00:17:19.829
And that caught on, and people
thought, hey, this is neat.

00:17:19.829 --> 00:17:21.300
Great.

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:24.270
And this led to
something called go/meet

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:27.440
that we were using
internally at Google in 2009.

00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:29.520
And this is another prototype.

00:17:29.520 --> 00:17:32.370
Arthur has not aged--

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:34.020
lucky him.

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:36.480
And you'll see, some other
people on that screen

00:17:36.480 --> 00:17:40.260
are in the room with us today.

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:43.860
And we launched this to Google
in 2009, and it became--

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:44.490
it picked up.

00:17:44.490 --> 00:17:46.170
It became a big success.

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:47.610
People could work from home.

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:51.330
The latency was a lot lower
than those video bridges

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:53.310
that had to transcode.

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:56.760
There was no transcoding
in this system.

00:17:56.760 --> 00:18:00.180
And people could take
meetings from their desk.

00:18:00.180 --> 00:18:01.800
They could take
meetings from home.

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:04.050
They could take
meetings on the road.

00:18:04.050 --> 00:18:10.620
And so there was a moment where
this started to grow quicker,

00:18:10.620 --> 00:18:13.620
and the Tandberg rooms were
being used less and less.

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:17.670
And this became Google
Hangouts a little bit later on.

00:18:20.540 --> 00:18:24.620
One thing that was different
with this back in 2009

00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:28.610
is, video was on by
default. You went

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:32.240
into a meeting, go/meet
hangout, or go/meet/foo,

00:18:32.240 --> 00:18:34.270
and video was on.

00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:37.910
And that was an important pillar
for this software experience,

00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:41.420
because if all these
people would join,

00:18:41.420 --> 00:18:43.460
and you'd all be
sort of avatars,

00:18:43.460 --> 00:18:47.060
and be audio only, who's
the first person to dare

00:18:47.060 --> 00:18:50.881
show themselves on camera in
front of all these others,

00:18:50.881 --> 00:18:51.380
right?

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:53.420
Who's that conceited
person that's

00:18:53.420 --> 00:18:55.700
going to be the first one
to say, hey, I'm so great,

00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:58.680
I'm going to show
myself for everyone.

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:00.780
And so those
systems didn't work.

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:02.840
The ones that didn't
have video on by default,

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:04.298
straight from the
start, they don't

00:19:04.298 --> 00:19:07.850
work for building team culture.

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:11.300
This created a sense of team.

00:19:11.300 --> 00:19:13.370
And after a few
times, you don't care

00:19:13.370 --> 00:19:16.220
about your hair or the mess the
kids made in the background,

00:19:16.220 --> 00:19:17.330
because this is your team.

00:19:17.330 --> 00:19:18.390
This is your safe space.

00:19:18.390 --> 00:19:20.390
These are the people
you create with.

00:19:20.390 --> 00:19:23.010
This is a people you
build software with.

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:33.340
Our second innovation
track was about the stack.

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:36.010
Building video
communication software

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:39.100
was a real pain back then.

00:19:39.100 --> 00:19:41.950
You had to license
third-party software.

00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:43.900
Not just surf
third-party technology,

00:19:43.900 --> 00:19:48.850
but a third-party audio stack,
a third-party video stack.

00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:52.360
And then you had the
fun part of taking

00:19:52.360 --> 00:19:55.210
two different technologies
from two different companies,

00:19:55.210 --> 00:19:57.850
put them on top of
your own network stack,

00:19:57.850 --> 00:20:00.550
and then fit this
into a plug-in.

00:20:00.550 --> 00:20:04.780
Remember, the plug-in
architecture is called NPAPI.

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:06.185
The N stands for Netscape.

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:12.020
Just, this is something
people forget.

00:20:12.020 --> 00:20:15.530
And you needed the plug-in
for Mac, Windows, and Linux.

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:17.510
And you needed to
have a great flow

00:20:17.510 --> 00:20:19.550
for deploying these
plug-ins, but also

00:20:19.550 --> 00:20:21.140
for keeping them updated.

00:20:21.140 --> 00:20:22.970
So how do you update a plug-in?

00:20:22.970 --> 00:20:26.240
How do you download the plug-in
so it doesn't affect the call?

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:28.550
How do you update the plug-in
so you don't tear down

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:30.230
a call that's going on?

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:32.030
And how do you do
that in a browser

00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:34.902
without the actual software
page being showed up,

00:20:34.902 --> 00:20:36.860
because that's usually
where these things would

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:38.420
get triggered?

00:20:38.420 --> 00:20:42.980
Very tricky, and with one
of the best install flows

00:20:42.980 --> 00:20:45.230
that you could find
on the internet,

00:20:45.230 --> 00:20:49.070
we still had first
attempts to join a meeting

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:51.200
failures of about 30%.

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:57.050
People just cannot-- it's just
too hard to download software

00:20:57.050 --> 00:21:01.250
on your desktop, and find
that file, run the installer,

00:21:01.250 --> 00:21:04.310
complete the installer,
remember to restart the browser,

00:21:04.310 --> 00:21:05.990
because this is a plug-in.

00:21:05.990 --> 00:21:09.980
People would fail at joining
their meetings the first time.

00:21:09.980 --> 00:21:11.870
All this complexity
of deployment,

00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:14.420
and having to integrate other
people and other companies'

00:21:14.420 --> 00:21:17.570
technology, meant that
innovation stagnated,

00:21:17.570 --> 00:21:20.480
and quality was a
hard thing to improve,

00:21:20.480 --> 00:21:23.310
when you're not in
control of your own stack.

00:21:23.310 --> 00:21:29.740
So a team at Google started
a project called WebRTC.

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:35.330
WebRTC is an open-source
audio and video platform

00:21:35.330 --> 00:21:38.510
that developers can
use to create video

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:41.340
and audio communication apps.

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:43.596
It's available on
Chrome, Android, and iOS,

00:21:43.596 --> 00:21:45.470
and there's other browsers
that take the code

00:21:45.470 --> 00:21:48.530
and implement it in
their own browsers.

00:21:48.530 --> 00:21:52.460
It's an open-source
community effort

00:21:52.460 --> 00:21:54.350
with different standard bodies.

00:21:54.350 --> 00:21:56.690
It's highly secure.

00:21:56.690 --> 00:21:59.410
And in Chrome, this is
a one-click experience.

00:21:59.410 --> 00:22:00.650
There's no more download.

00:22:00.650 --> 00:22:03.890
You click, and you're
in your meeting.

00:22:03.890 --> 00:22:07.160
That helped a lot of users.

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:11.200
And it's not a stretch
of the imagination

00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:14.740
to say that WebRTC,
today, powers video

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:16.130
conferencing on the internet.

00:22:16.130 --> 00:22:20.630
If you use Google Hangouts,
Google Duo, Facebook Messenger,

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:27.460
GoToMeeting, Twilio, the list
of companies is over 1,000 long,

00:22:27.460 --> 00:22:29.890
of companies that
integrate WebRTC,

00:22:29.890 --> 00:22:33.010
the code that Google
wrote and Google

00:22:33.010 --> 00:22:35.800
made available
for creating web--

00:22:35.800 --> 00:22:37.790
for creating video
conferencing experiences.

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:45.270
So now we had started
building software.

00:22:45.270 --> 00:22:48.750
Then we decided that we
wanted to own the stack.

00:22:48.750 --> 00:22:51.180
We wanted to understand
how audio and video works.

00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:54.210
We wanted to make
that part better.

00:22:54.210 --> 00:22:57.000
But we still had
Google Chrome growing

00:22:57.000 --> 00:23:00.690
like crazy in all these
meeting rooms that needed

00:23:00.690 --> 00:23:04.230
videoconferencing support.

00:23:04.230 --> 00:23:09.720
And so this is a video
of two of my colleagues,

00:23:09.720 --> 00:23:13.590
Steve [INAUDIBLE]
and Eric [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:13.590 --> 00:23:16.800
We bought a HP
TouchSmart in 2008,

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:18.960
and that's the one we bought.

00:23:18.960 --> 00:23:21.660
This ran Windows Vista.

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:25.140
It ran Firefox and something
called Greasemonkey,

00:23:25.140 --> 00:23:28.550
if any of you remember this.

00:23:28.550 --> 00:23:31.050
It would grab the video
chat little bubble

00:23:31.050 --> 00:23:33.630
that you saw in
that video, and it

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:36.930
would make it big so that you
didn't know Gmail was there

00:23:36.930 --> 00:23:38.059
in the background.

00:23:38.059 --> 00:23:39.600
And with the touch
screen, it allowed

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:41.590
you to punch in a meeting name.

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:48.641
It cost $1,000, $1,050,
instead of several thousands

00:23:48.641 --> 00:23:49.140
of dollars.

00:23:49.140 --> 00:23:51.270
There were no remote
controls or devices.

00:23:51.270 --> 00:23:52.920
This was an all-in-one.

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:54.420
And so this was
our first attempt

00:23:54.420 --> 00:24:01.360
at creating a video conferencing
room setup by ourselves.

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:02.080
Oh, I will--

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:02.830
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:24:02.830 --> 00:24:04.080
- --with Eric [INAUDIBLE],
who's also filming.

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:04.205
- Hi.

00:24:04.205 --> 00:24:04.705
- Hi.

00:24:04.705 --> 00:24:07.210
We're working on the
[INAUDIBLE] project

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:10.930
to kiosk a PC to function
as a Tandberg replacement.

00:24:10.930 --> 00:24:12.867
So I'm going to go
ahead and power it on.

00:24:12.867 --> 00:24:14.700
As soon as you hit dial,
it will go directly

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:18.810
to this page that will show
you a video conference that you

00:24:18.810 --> 00:24:22.230
will join in progress.

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:24.390
- This was one of the
first times it all worked.

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:25.640
- Let's see if anyone's there.

00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:26.310
Calling--

00:24:26.310 --> 00:24:27.452
- Ta-da.

00:24:27.452 --> 00:24:28.410
- Just like talk video.

00:24:28.410 --> 00:24:30.330
[END PLAYBACK]

00:24:30.330 --> 00:24:32.280
SERGE LACHAPELLE:
And that was it.

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:34.942
The actual meeting-- the actual
video was like 15 minutes

00:24:34.942 --> 00:24:36.525
long, because it was
powered by Vista,

00:24:36.525 --> 00:24:39.370
and it took so
much time to boot.

00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:43.980
But so, you know, the team
did-- there was so much

00:24:43.980 --> 00:24:46.980
work that needed to be
done after this first proof

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:47.820
of concept, right?

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:51.450
We moved this to Linux,
we redid the front end,

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:57.230
we had to do fleet
management on this

00:24:57.230 --> 00:25:00.330
so that we-- because we wanted
to deploy thousands of these.

00:25:00.330 --> 00:25:03.930
But that little video,
those two individuals

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:07.410
and the others that worked
with them, led to a revolution

00:25:07.410 --> 00:25:09.780
at Google.

00:25:09.780 --> 00:25:12.050
We built a video
conferencing appliance.

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:20.760
And so this is today at Google.

00:25:20.760 --> 00:25:25.170
And all of our meeting rooms
have video conferencing.

00:25:25.170 --> 00:25:29.790
And they're all built on
top of the same software

00:25:29.790 --> 00:25:33.540
that I just talked about,
Hangouts and WebRTC.

00:25:33.540 --> 00:25:39.150
It's powered by
computers that run--

00:25:39.150 --> 00:25:42.660
that run ChromeOS today.

00:25:42.660 --> 00:25:44.860
We're going to talk about
that in a little minute.

00:25:44.860 --> 00:25:48.870
But we've got over
15,000 rooms today

00:25:48.870 --> 00:25:51.540
that have video
conferencing capabilities.

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:55.380
So this is one of the
offices in Tel Aviv.

00:25:55.380 --> 00:25:56.670
Here is an office in London.

00:25:56.670 --> 00:25:58.260
This is a smaller huddle room.

00:26:01.960 --> 00:26:07.360
And this is how good we've
become at choosing the cables,

00:26:07.360 --> 00:26:09.280
passing the cabling--

00:26:09.280 --> 00:26:11.950
the amount of
discipline and thought

00:26:11.950 --> 00:26:14.440
that has gone
through all of this

00:26:14.440 --> 00:26:17.980
is just staggering, because
when you have to do something

00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:25.120
15,000 times across 70 offices
now, or 70 countries now,

00:26:25.120 --> 00:26:27.340
you can't let
anything to chance.

00:26:27.340 --> 00:26:28.750
So what cables do you use?

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:30.610
How do you pull the cables?

00:26:30.610 --> 00:26:32.440
What holes do you drill?

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:36.270
What boxes that you choose to
insulate sound from the PC?

00:26:36.270 --> 00:26:38.590
All those things are
really, really important.

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:44.650
And we put that ubiquity to use.

00:26:44.650 --> 00:26:47.620
So today, if you look
at our usage at Google,

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:52.290
we've got Googlers dialing in
to a video meeting 230,000 times

00:26:52.290 --> 00:26:57.480
a day for a company that's
about 71,000 employees.

00:26:57.480 --> 00:27:04.890
We spend 9.2, 9.3 years of time
every day in video meetings.

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:06.510
And this is not just meetings.

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:09.660
This is, again, a team
that is distributed,

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:12.810
that wants to feel that
feeling of togetherness.

00:27:12.810 --> 00:27:15.910
It's people that want
to have one-on-ones.

00:27:15.910 --> 00:27:19.440
It's teams that want to
have their daily Scrums,

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:20.520
or their huddles.

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:23.040
And it's people that just want
to have a virtual presence.

00:27:23.040 --> 00:27:25.410
Maybe they're working
from home that week,

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:27.200
and they want to feel
close to their team,

00:27:27.200 --> 00:27:29.730
so they're just on video,
and the rest of the team

00:27:29.730 --> 00:27:31.350
follows suit.

00:27:31.350 --> 00:27:35.310
And again, we've got over
15,000 of these rooms

00:27:35.310 --> 00:27:37.170
internally at Google.

00:27:37.170 --> 00:27:41.760
And then everyone has their
laptops and their phones,

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:44.550
and the same experience
that they have in the room

00:27:44.550 --> 00:27:47.160
moves to the laptop
and moves to the phone.

00:27:47.160 --> 00:27:51.300
That's a very important
detail in this case,

00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:54.810
because you don't want to
create islands of communication.

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:56.370
If you have a
different room system

00:27:56.370 --> 00:27:58.286
than what you use on
your laptop and than what

00:27:58.286 --> 00:28:01.590
you use on your phone, when
you try to bring all of these

00:28:01.590 --> 00:28:05.580
together, you create
kind of separate islands

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:08.880
of collaboration, and the
lowest common denominator

00:28:08.880 --> 00:28:11.760
of the experience--

00:28:11.760 --> 00:28:16.320
you know, it just makes
everything dumbed down a lot.

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:22.990
And I don't know what happened,
but you see, the company,

00:28:22.990 --> 00:28:26.560
since 2013, doubled in size.

00:28:26.560 --> 00:28:29.755
But the video minutes in
this system have gone up 17x.

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:39.020
So as the company grows, you've
got this kind of social graph

00:28:39.020 --> 00:28:40.220
that just explodes.

00:28:40.220 --> 00:28:45.110
And people want to share
experiences, share knowledge,

00:28:45.110 --> 00:28:49.430
and just make sure that
everyone is up to date.

00:28:49.430 --> 00:28:54.300
And the whole company can make
decisions quicker thanks to it.

00:28:59.190 --> 00:29:03.060
And I think this is what makes
Google one of the-- a unique

00:29:03.060 --> 00:29:06.330
company, because we can
do global engineering,

00:29:06.330 --> 00:29:10.800
and I think you can
see it taking off now.

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:15.760
There is a fourth
track to all of this,

00:29:15.760 --> 00:29:20.740
and it's a little product
called Chromebox for Meetings.

00:29:20.740 --> 00:29:23.980
The same team that built
the beautiful deployments

00:29:23.980 --> 00:29:28.450
that you saw a few years
ago understood that ubiquity

00:29:28.450 --> 00:29:30.380
was very important.

00:29:30.380 --> 00:29:33.010
They wanted to be able
to scale things up

00:29:33.010 --> 00:29:39.900
to much more than 15,000 rooms.

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:42.120
I've heard a rumor that
there's expectations

00:29:42.120 --> 00:29:45.780
that we had an extra 20,000
rooms over the next two years

00:29:45.780 --> 00:29:47.820
to our system.

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:50.400
And that team
understood very quickly

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:56.840
that having a computer, having
to pass all these wires,

00:29:56.840 --> 00:29:59.280
maybe was a level
of complexity that

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:02.170
was too high to be able to
scale up to those numbers.

00:30:02.170 --> 00:30:04.920
So that team sat
down and created

00:30:04.920 --> 00:30:08.760
a system that would also
cater to our customers' needs,

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:12.960
because our customers have as
large deployments as we do.

00:30:12.960 --> 00:30:15.870
Some of our customers beat
us in the amount of minutes

00:30:15.870 --> 00:30:18.630
they do per employee out there.

00:30:18.630 --> 00:30:22.586
It's incredible to see the
power of ubiquitous video

00:30:22.586 --> 00:30:26.270
conferencing and what
it brings to a company.

00:30:26.270 --> 00:30:29.820
A ChromeOS machine
that is secure--

00:30:29.820 --> 00:30:32.250
there is nothing more secure
than a ChromeOS computer

00:30:32.250 --> 00:30:33.840
on the market today
because of the way

00:30:33.840 --> 00:30:38.790
the hardware is signed into
the software and vice versa.

00:30:38.790 --> 00:30:42.000
A computer that allows you
to do remote management of it

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:43.380
very easily.

00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:46.140
And this is why Chromebooks
are so popular in schools

00:30:46.140 --> 00:30:49.260
and in enterprise, because
of this remote management

00:30:49.260 --> 00:30:51.370
capabilities.

00:30:51.370 --> 00:30:54.240
A computer that connects
to your calendar,

00:30:54.240 --> 00:30:57.090
to your room's calendar, and
all it does all day is it

00:30:57.090 --> 00:31:00.579
shows your room's calendar,
and it's waiting for a click.

00:31:00.579 --> 00:31:02.370
Its just sitting there
waiting for a click.

00:31:02.370 --> 00:31:04.980
And when someone clicks,
boom, you're in the meeting.

00:31:04.980 --> 00:31:08.850
And that's about the amount
of interaction it gets.

00:31:08.850 --> 00:31:10.500
When you share-- when
you want to share

00:31:10.500 --> 00:31:12.630
a document to your
meeting, you do it

00:31:12.630 --> 00:31:13.980
over Wi-Fi from your computer.

00:31:18.960 --> 00:31:21.016
You can mute yourself,
you can hang up the call,

00:31:21.016 --> 00:31:22.140
and you can start the call.

00:31:22.140 --> 00:31:24.870
And everything comes
from Google Calendar.

00:31:24.870 --> 00:31:27.630
So it integrates really
well with G Suite.

00:31:27.630 --> 00:31:30.330
And we really believe
that this is the next step

00:31:30.330 --> 00:31:33.180
to our ubiquity project.

00:31:33.180 --> 00:31:36.060
And we're working really
hard at evolving this,

00:31:36.060 --> 00:31:40.500
not only for growing Google's
video conferencing culture,

00:31:40.500 --> 00:31:44.730
but also bringing our customers
with us on the journey.

00:31:44.730 --> 00:31:48.900
So expect a lot of great things
on this front a little bit

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:49.680
later this year.

00:31:53.000 --> 00:32:00.220
So finally, today, you
could say that thanks

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:04.900
to all this work, and all
this innovation, and 10 years

00:32:04.900 --> 00:32:10.640
of sweat and tears, we've
been able to support a video--

00:32:10.640 --> 00:32:16.930
a global engineering culture
that fits into what Google is.

00:32:16.930 --> 00:32:18.280
It spans the globe.

00:32:18.280 --> 00:32:19.840
It saves time.

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:22.850
It helps Google make
decisions quickly.

00:32:22.850 --> 00:32:26.170
It helps people share
ideas, help each other,

00:32:26.170 --> 00:32:29.770
and be able to try to reach
some kind of work-life balance.

00:32:29.770 --> 00:32:32.410
If you need to work from
home, you work from home.

00:32:32.410 --> 00:32:35.440
There's no need to
commute in every day.

00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:39.430
And now, this is a
tool that is evolving

00:32:39.430 --> 00:32:41.620
based on your
feedback and the input

00:32:41.620 --> 00:32:45.790
that you've been
giving us as customers.

00:32:45.790 --> 00:32:50.050
And you might wonder what
happened to our Tandberg

00:32:50.050 --> 00:32:51.460
systems.

00:32:51.460 --> 00:32:53.760
And when I was looking
at this last night,

00:32:53.760 --> 00:32:57.370
I found a web page from
a team in Australia

00:32:57.370 --> 00:33:04.020
that recycled them and created
PlayStation 4 kiosks with them.

00:33:04.020 --> 00:33:07.240
And apparently, the
shape of the PS4

00:33:07.240 --> 00:33:10.960
fit perfectly in that
cabinet, and they

00:33:10.960 --> 00:33:12.880
became very popular
in certain countries

00:33:12.880 --> 00:33:16.930
as PlayStation 4 enablers.

00:33:16.930 --> 00:33:19.045
So on that, thanks
for listening.

00:33:19.045 --> 00:33:21.780
[MUSIC PLAYING]

